The C# programming language also doesn't support raw assertions in its syntax like C++ does, but it has a `System.Diagnostics.Debug` class with a `Assert` method that we can use.

The problem is about parsing the source code and creating assert statements based on comments, so you can use a parsing library like `SyntaxTree` from `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis` namespace that can be used to parse C# code.

However, to stay simple, assuming that our code is properly formatted with braces and `//` for single line comments, this problem can be solved easily in a text replacement process as follows:

```
// The following lines should be executed in a csharp file
var lines = File.ReadAllLines("YourCSharpFileName.cs");

for (int i = 0; i < lines.Length; i++)
{
    if (lines[i].StartsWith("// @@@ "))
    {
        var comment = lines[i].Replace("// ", "");
        var statement = comment.Replace(" ", ".Assert(") +  " == true);\n";
        lines[i] = statement;
    }
}

File.WriteAllLines("output.cs", lines);
```

This will create an `output.cs` file with all the comments replaced by debug assertions. It's just a simple example and won't work with complex logic inside the comments. It might need to be extended depending on the specific needs of your use case.--------------------
